; SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD
; sand.source.vla - Sand, a falling-sand toy on a 320 by 200 plane, as its author writes
; it (CART.2): the first game, and a fixture of the engine's floors beside Life. The pass
; expands this file into sand.vla beside it, the rows the engine loads (SPEC.md section
; 7.8): edit this one, then expand it again with
;   cargo run --release --example expand
;
; How to play: paint with the left button, erase with the right, and turn the wheel to
; size the brush, a disc of radius 1 to 8 (3 at the start). Press 1 for sand, 3 for water,
; 2 for wall and 0 for empty; the floor shows the material the left button paints. The toy
; opens raining sand and water: its first frame is Life's soup over the board's top third,
; three eighths of it as grains, half of them water, landing in about two seconds.
;
; The board is the Screen itself. The interior, C2:LF199, is 316 by 198 cells; the walls
; two cells wide down each side, A:B and LG:LH, since a grain's second choice reads two
; columns aside; row 1 empty above the interior, so that nothing falls out of it; row
; 200 the floor. The codes: 0 empty, 1 wall, 2 sand and 3 water.
;
; The rule is a scan down the board in three sheets, each one fill over the interior:
;   - Stay says whether a cell's occupant stays after its row's grains have fallen: 0 for
;     a free cell, 1 for a wall or a grain of sand that stays, 2 for water. Its row reads
;     Side's row beneath in this frame, and the Screen's last frame.
;   - Side says whether the cell is occupied after its row's water has moved sideways. Its
;     row reads Stay's same row in this frame, and nothing of the last frame (rule 7).
;   - Screen holds each cell's material at the frame's end. Its row reads Stay's and
;     Side's same row in this frame, and the Screen's last frame, its own row and the row
;     above.
; A grain falls straight down when the cell beneath is free; else to its first diagonal,
; when that is free and no grain falls straight into it; else to the other diagonal, when
; that is free and taken neither straight nor by the grain whose first choice it is. The
; first diagonal alternates by row, right first on an even row, so of the two grains that
; could take a cell on a diagonal exactly one has it as its first choice: a fixed
; priority settles every claim. Water falls as sand does, and a grain of water that
; stays then moves one cell sideways, into a cell of its row left free, its row's
; direction first; of two waters that could take a cell, the one whose first choice it
; is takes it. The receiving cell chooses, computing the grain's own move again, so a
; grain moves at most once a frame and is never doubled or lost. Sand and water fall
; alike, and neither sinks through the other.
;
; Water never settles. A grain beside a free cell of its row moves into it, and the next
; frame its first side is free again, so it moves back: a lake's edge shimmers, and a
; board holding water is never still. Once the pouring stops, the shimmer costs 0.2 to
; 0.5 ms a frame in the browser, since a frame computes only the cells a change reaches.
;
; What a rule costs, as Sand's scoping measured it (REARVIEW.md, CART.2, decisions 2 and
; 23; the rules are SPEC.md section 2's note of 2026-10-11), and which this source keeps:
;   1. A sheet reads itself this frame only in the row beneath. Another sheet's same row
;      is free. A row that reads the row beside collapses its run to one lane.
;   2. One fill a formula. The same rule written a fill a row made 398 shapes, and a
;      frame's start took 369 ms of a 387 ms frame.
;   3. No term that changes every frame, and no raw pointer, in a cell's formula. Each
;      goes through a cell that holds still when idle, or every frame is whole.
;   4. A test that is rarely true reads a cell of its own row, the brush's Gate, so that a
;      press changes only the rows under the brush. Behind a part every $ holds, the guard
;      saved 24% of the worst frame, but its flip at every press and release, and the
;      material held at every cell at every key, computed all 189,017 cells, 17 to 26 ms
;      (the playtest pass, 2026-10-12).
;   5. A value fixed for the grid's life is computed once and read through its twin,
;      not called at each cell: the Parity column, 15%. No CHOOSE in a board's formula:
;      +38%. (Since vla-lang 0.9.1 a run computes MOD, ROW and CHOOSE across its cells,
;      and the twin is about 1% cheaper; the column stays.)
;   6. A class of materials is one comparison: what moves is >=2.
;   7. A fact a later sheet needs is carried in the value of a sheet it already reads,
;      not read again from the twin: Stay says 2 for water, so Side reads Stay alone.
;      Each reference is a reader marked at every change, in a branch no cell takes as
;      well: Side reading the last frame beside Stay made every board two thirds dearer.

; The library whose macros this source calls: palette, cells and formulas.
(include "../common.vla")

(cartridge "sand"
  (spec 3)                          ; the version of SPEC.md this cartridge is written to
  (title "Sand")                    ; what the host shows
  (rate 60)                         ; frames a second: a grain moves a cell a frame
  (screen plane 320 200)            ; plane mode, 320 cells wide and 200 high: A1:LH200
  (seed 1)                          ; the dice, Clock!B3: the soup of the first frame
  (author "Spreadsheet Company")    ; who wrote it
  (licence "0BSD")                  ; the header's twin: copy this file to start a cartridge of your own
  (notes "A falling-sand toy. Paint with the left button, erase with the right, and turn the wheel to size the brush; press 1 for sand, 3 for water, 2 for wall and 0 for empty. The floor shows what the left button paints. It opens raining sand and water from its top third, Life's first frame as grains."))

; The sheets, in tab order: the Screen the rule draws on, and Stay and Side beside it;
; Parity, the direction of each row; the brush, its Gate and the material in State; the
; Tally of each row; the Palette.
(sheet "Screen" visible)
(sheet "Stay" visible)
(sheet "Side" visible)
(sheet "Parity" visible)
(sheet "Brush" visible)
(sheet "Gate" visible)
(sheet "State" visible)
(sheet "Tally" visible)
(sheet "Palette" visible)

; ---- the Palette (SPEC.md section 3.2): the codes first ------------------------------
; Index 0 is empty, 1 a wall, 2 sand and 3 water, so that what moves is the codes from 2
; up, one comparison. The CGA's colours follow for a modder to use.
(palette                            ; the sixteen colours, index 0 first
  ("#000000" "empty")               ; 0, black
  ("#808080" "wall")                ; 1, grey
  ("#E8C872" "sand")                ; 2
  ("#3060E0" "water")               ; 3
  ("#0000AA" "blue")                ; 4
  ("#00AA00" "green")               ; 5
  ("#00AAAA" "cyan")                ; 6
  ("#AA0000" "red")                 ; 7
  ("#AA00AA" "magenta")             ; 8
  ("#AA5500" "brown")               ; 9
  ("#AAAAAA" "light grey")          ; 10
  ("#5555FF" "light blue")          ; 11
  ("#55FF55" "light green")         ; 12
  ("#FF5555" "light red")           ; 13
  ("#FFFF55" "yellow")              ; 14
  ("#FFFFFF" "white"))              ; 15

; ---- the frame of the board: walls, and the border of Stay and Side -------------------
(cells "Screen"                     ; the walls, two cells wide, the full height
  ("A1:B200" 1)
  ("LG1:LH200" 1))
(cells "Stay"                       ; the border is occupied, so nothing moves into it
  ("A1:B200" 1)
  ("LG1:LH200" 1)
  ("C200:LF200" 1))                 ; the floor
(cells "Side"                       ; the same border
  ("A1:B200" 1)
  ("LG1:LH200" 1)
  ("C200:LF200" 1))

; ---- Parity: the direction of each row, computed once ---------------------------------
; Part of the rule, as the walls are: a grain goes right first on an even row and left
; first on an odd one, and an edit that breaks the alternation lets two grains claim one
; cell. The rule reads it through its twin, Parity.last, an index into an array, where
; MOD(ROW(),2) at every cell would be a call at every cell (rule 5).
(formula "Parity" "A1:A200" "=MOD(ROW(),2)")

; ---- the brush, entered only while a button is held -----------------------------------
(cells "Brush"                      ; the labels
  ("A1" "column")
  ("A2" "row")
  ("A3" "radius")
  ("A4" "material"))
(formulas "Brush"                   ; the only cells that read the pointer (rule 3)
  ; the pointer's column and row while the left or the right button is held over the
  ; Screen, and -999 otherwise, which no disc reaches
  ("B1" "=IF(AND(Input!B13+Input!B14>0,Input!B11>0,Input!B12>0),Input!B11,-999)")
  ("B2" "=IF(AND(Input!B13+Input!B14>0,Input!B11>0,Input!B12>0),Input!B12,-999)")
  ; the radius: 3 at the start, then the wheel's notches, larger away from the user
  ("B3" "=IF(Clock!B1<=1,3,MAX(1,MIN(8,Brush.last!B3-Input!B16)))")
  ; the right button paints empty, and the left the material picked
  ("B4" "=IF(Input!B14=1,0,State!B1)"))

; ---- the Gate: the disc, row by row ----------------------------------------------------
; For each row of the interior, the brush's column, the square of the disc's half-width
; there, and the material it paints; -999, -1 and -1 outside its rows, which no cell is
; inside. A cell of row r is inside the disc when Gate!Br is not negative and
; (COLUMN()-Gate!Ar)^2 <= Gate!Br. The rule reads the brush from this sheet alone, so a
; press, a release or a key changes only the rows under the disc (rule 4).
(formulas "Gate"
  ("A2:A199" "=IF((ROW()-Brush!$B$2)^2<=Brush!$B$3^2,Brush!$B$1,-999)")
  ("B2:B199" "=IF((ROW()-Brush!$B$2)^2<=Brush!$B$3^2,Brush!$B$3^2-(ROW()-Brush!$B$2)^2,-1)")
  ("C2:C199" "=IF((ROW()-Brush!$B$2)^2<=Brush!$B$3^2,Brush!$B$4,-1)"))

; ---- State: the material the left button paints ------------------------------------------
(cells "State"
  ("A1" "material"))
(formula "State" "B1"
  (&expand
    "=IF(Clock!$B$1<=1,2,"                  ; sand at the start
    "IF(Input!$B$17=\"1\",2,"               ; 1: sand
    "IF(Input!$B$17=\"2\",1,"               ; 2: wall
    "IF(Input!$B$17=\"3\",3,"               ; 3: water
    "IF(Input!$B$17=\"0\",0,"               ; 0: empty
    "State.last!$B$1)))))"))                ; any other key, or none: kept

; ---- Stay: whether the cell's occupant stays after its row's grains have fallen -------
; Written at C2, the fill's first cell: Side!C3 is the cell beneath after its row's water
; has moved sideways this frame, and Screen.last!A2:E2 the row's grains as the last frame
; left them. An occupant that stays says 1, or 2 when it is water, so that Side, which
; moves the water, reads this sheet alone (rule 7).
(formula "Stay" "C2:LF199"
  (&expand
    "=IF(IF(Gate!$B2<0,FALSE,"                                     ; the brush, tested only in its rows (rule 4):
    "(COLUMN()-Gate!$A2)^2<=Gate!$B2),"                            ;   the cell inside the disc, by its row's gate,
    "IF(Gate!$C2<>0,IF(Screen.last!C2=3,2,1),0),"                  ;   is occupied when the paint is not empty, 2 over water, whose grain still moves;
    "IF(Screen.last!C2=1,1,"                                       ; else a wall stays,
    "IF(AND(Screen.last!C2>=2,Side!C3=1),"                         ; and a grain whose cell beneath is taken
    "IF(Parity.last!$A2=0,"                                        ; on an even row, right first:
    "IF(OR(AND(Side!D3=0,Screen.last!D2<2),"                       ;   leaves for its first diagonal, free and none falling into it,
    "AND(Side!B3=0,Screen.last!B2<2,"                              ;   or else for its second, free and none falling into it,
    "OR(Screen.last!A2<2,Side!A3=0))),0,"                          ;   when the grain two to the left does not take it first;
    "IF(Screen.last!C2=3,2,1)),"                                   ;   else stays, 2 for water and 1 for sand.
    "IF(OR(AND(Side!B3=0,Screen.last!B2<2),"                       ; on an odd row, left first, the same mirrored:
    "AND(Side!D3=0,Screen.last!D2<2,"
    "OR(Screen.last!E2<2,Side!E3=0))),0,"
    "IF(Screen.last!C2=3,2,1))),"
    "0)))"))                                                       ; an empty cell, or a grain that falls straight, does not stay

; ---- Side: whether the cell is occupied after its row's water has moved sideways ------
; Written at C2: Stay!A2:E2 is the row after its grains have fallen. Water that stays
; moves one cell sideways, into a cell of its row left free: first the side its row's
; direction names, then the other, when the water two cells away does not take that
; cell first. A free cell is taken by the water whose first choice it is, or else by the
; water whose second choice it is, when that water's first is not free. The brush
; paints this sheet as it paints Stay.
(formula "Side" "C2:LF199"
  (&expand
    "=IF(IF(Gate!$B2<0,FALSE,"                                     ; the brush, as Stay tests it:
    "(COLUMN()-Gate!$A2)^2<=Gate!$B2),"
    "IF(Gate!$C2<>0,1,0),"                                         ;   occupied when the paint is not empty;
    "IF(Stay!C2=2,"                                                ; else water that stays
    "IF(IF(Parity.last!$A2=0,"                                     ;   on an even row, right first:
    "OR(Stay!D2=0,AND(Stay!B2=0,Stay!A2<>2)),"                     ;   leaves for a free cell right, or left when the water two to the left does not take it;
    "OR(Stay!B2=0,AND(Stay!D2=0,Stay!E2<>2))),0,1),"               ;   on an odd row, mirrored; else it stays;
    "IF(Stay!C2=1,1,"                                              ; a wall or a grain of sand that stays is there;
    "IF(IF(Parity.last!$A2=0,"                                     ; and a free cell, on an even row,
    "OR(Stay!B2=2,AND(Stay!D2=2,Stay!E2>0)),"                      ;   takes the water on its left, or the water on its right when that one's right is taken;
    "OR(Stay!D2=2,AND(Stay!B2=2,Stay!A2>0))),1,0))))"))            ;   on an odd row, mirrored; else it stays free.

; ---- Screen: the material at the frame's end -------------------------------------------
; Written at C2: Stay!A2:E2 and Side!A2:E2 are the row after its grains have fallen and
; after its water has moved sideways, Screen.last!A1:E1 the grains above as the last frame
; left them. The first frame is Life's soup as grains, by Life's own arithmetic and
; threshold: two linear forms of the seed, ROW() and COLUMN() reduced modulo the prime
; P = 67108859 and multiplied, h = MOD(a*b, P), and a grain where MOD(h*h, P) < 25165822,
; three eighths of P (life.source.vla says why it is exact): sand where it is under
; 12582911, three sixteenths of P, and water above that, so half the grains are water.
; The soup is computed twice, once for each threshold, in the first frame alone. It rains
; from the top third, rows 2 to 67: the whole board falling at once made every frame of
; its first two seconds take two of a 60 Hz display's ticks, and a third of it takes one
; (the playtest pass, 2026-10-12).
(formula "Screen" "C2:LF199"
  (&expand
    "=IF(Clock!$B$1=1,"                                                      ; the first frame:
    "IF(ROW()>67,0,"                                                         ;   nothing below the top third,
    "IF(MOD(MOD(MOD(Clock!$B$3*1103515+ROW()*1597334+COLUMN()*1830421+12345,67108859)"
    "*MOD(Clock!$B$3*2012345+ROW()*1209487+COLUMN()*1949213+54321,67108859),67108859)"
    "*MOD(MOD(Clock!$B$3*1103515+ROW()*1597334+COLUMN()*1830421+12345,67108859)"
    "*MOD(Clock!$B$3*2012345+ROW()*1209487+COLUMN()*1949213+54321,67108859),67108859),"
    "67108859)<12582911,2,"                                                 ;   sand where the soup's square is under three sixteenths,
    "IF(MOD(MOD(MOD(Clock!$B$3*1103515+ROW()*1597334+COLUMN()*1830421+12345,67108859)"
    "*MOD(Clock!$B$3*2012345+ROW()*1209487+COLUMN()*1949213+54321,67108859),67108859)"
    "*MOD(MOD(Clock!$B$3*1103515+ROW()*1597334+COLUMN()*1830421+12345,67108859)"
    "*MOD(Clock!$B$3*2012345+ROW()*1209487+COLUMN()*1949213+54321,67108859),67108859),"
    "67108859)<25165822,3,0))),"                                            ;   water where it is under three eighths
    "IF(IF(Gate!$B2<0,FALSE,"                                                ; the brush, as Stay tests it:
    "(COLUMN()-Gate!$A2)^2<=Gate!$B2),"
    "Gate!$C2,"                                                              ;   the paint
    "IF(Side!C2=1,IF(Stay!C2>0,Screen.last!C2,3),"                           ; else an occupant that stayed keeps its cell, and water moved in is water;
    "IF(Screen.last!C1>=2,Screen.last!C1,"                                   ; a free cell takes the grain falling straight,
    "IF(Parity.last!$A1=0,"                                                  ; and when the row above goes right first,
    "IF(AND(Screen.last!B1>=2,Side!B2=1),Screen.last!B1,"                    ;   the grain up left, its first choice,
    "IF(AND(Screen.last!D1>=2,Side!D2=1,"                                    ;   or the grain up right, its second choice,
    "OR(Side!E2=1,Screen.last!E1>=2)),Screen.last!D1,0)),"                   ;   when its first is not open; else empty;
    "IF(AND(Screen.last!D1>=2,Side!D2=1),Screen.last!D1,"                    ; and when it goes left first, mirrored
    "IF(AND(Screen.last!B1>=2,Side!B2=1,"
    "OR(Side!A2=1,Screen.last!A1>=2)),Screen.last!B1,0)))))))"))

; The floor shows the material the left button paints. No formula reads row 200 of the
; Screen, since Stay's and Side's row 200 is what the row above it stands on.
(formula "Screen" "C200:LF200" "=State!$B$1")

; ---- the Tally: each board row counted, from the twins --------------------------------
; Column A the sum of the row's codes, column B its occupied cells, read from the last
; frame's Screen and Side; on a board where nothing falls, B is the row's grains and
; walls, and A less B its sand and twice its water. A frame's end is the replay's golden
; (decision 12).
(cells "Tally"
  ("A1" "codes")
  ("B1" "occupied"))
(formulas "Tally"
  ("A2:A199" "=SUM(Screen.last!C2:LF2)")
  ("B2:B199" "=SUM(Side.last!C2:LF2)"))
